# wfg-log 1
# fixture manual-descent — the pointer walks into two nested manual groups and
# out again, hand-written and hand-checked.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXTURE IS FOR. §3.6 makes the OPERATOR the parent of a manual
# sequence group: the standby pointer descends into it, each GO fires the member
# the pointer has reached, and the pointer climbs out again the moment the last
# member has been fired. Every one of those decisions is the scheduler's, and a
# scheduler decides in a tick hook — which `wfg replay` does not run. So each
# decision leaves as a command, and this file is what that buys: the whole
# descent reproduces from the records below, with no hook in the room.
#
# It also carries the case that was actually wrong. A GO on a member inside a
# group inside a group has to create both of those groups before it can be their
# child, and for a while it created one of them twice — two runs of the inner
# group, two jobs, and the member fired twice from one press. Nothing caught it,
# because the only test that went two levels deep looked at the shape of the GO
# record and not at what ran.
#
# It needs the bundle because GO reads standby out of the show, and it needs no
# audio side: `Audio tracks="0"` is a real answer, and a show of memos is what a
# designer writes on a train.
#
# THE POINTER STARTS THREE LEVELS DOWN, on `DDDD6666`, restored from state.xml —
# which is legal exactly because every group above it is a manual sequence, and
# is how a show reopens where the operator left it.
#
# GO ONE creates a run for each of the two groups the pointer is inside, in the
# order they were made, outermost first. That is why this record is variadic: a
# replay never draws an identifier of its own, so it has to be handed all of
# them. Only the outer group is STARTED here; the inner one is created and left
# standing, because its parent has a header to run first and starting it now
# would be the scene beginning before its own preparation.
A 0 0 cli go s:"RN000001" s:"RN000002"
#
# The outer group's header, spawned on the next tick. A header is an ordinary
# cue list run as a sequence whatever the group's own mode says, and it runs
# before any member.
A 1 1 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"HDR40000" s:"RN000003"
A 2 2 engine run.launch s:"RN000003"
#
# A memo ends on the tick after it fires — the same tick a network cue with
# `wait: none` ends on, because that is when a report is allowed to leave.
A 3 3 engine run.ended s:"RN000003"
#
# Tick 4 is the boundary the header's end buys: the members phase begins, and
# it begins at the member the pointer is inside of rather than at member one.
# That member is the inner GROUP, and its run already exists — GO made it — so
# it is adopted rather than spawned again. Nothing is submitted, which is why
# there is no record for that tick.
#
# Then the inner group is launched by its parent's job, which is the moment
# §3.6 puts it: after the header, and not before.
A 5 4 engine run.launch s:"RN000002"
#
# The inner group has no header, so its own members begin at once — at the cue
# the pointer was actually on.
A 6 5 engine run.spawn s:"RN000002" s:"DDDD6666" s:"RN000004"
A 7 6 engine run.launch s:"RN000004"
A 8 7 engine run.ended s:"RN000004"
#
# AND NOW NOTHING HAPPENS, for twelve ticks, which is the whole of what a manual
# group is. The member is done and the group is not: §3.6 says a member starts
# on GO and the operator is the parent, so the scene waits. A group that ended
# here would be indistinguishable from one that is simply idle between presses,
# and anything holding on it would be released early.
#
# GO TWO fires the second member of the inner group. No group is created — both
# are already running — so the record carries one identifier, the member's own.
# The pointer had already moved to this cue on the previous press.
A 20 8 cli go s:"RN000005"
A 21 9 engine run.launch s:"RN000005"
A 22 10 engine run.ended s:"RN000005"
#
# That was the LAST member of the inner group, so the inner group is over: its
# last member was started and every child of it has finished, which are two
# different questions and both have to be asked. It has no footer, so it reports
# done straight away.
A 23 11 engine run.ended s:"RN000002"
#
# The pointer left the inner group on the press that fired its last member —
# decision M, 2026-09-06: no GO is ever spent on leaving — so it is now on
# `FFFF8888`, the outer group's second member.
#
# GO THREE fires it, as a child of the outer group's run.
A 30 12 cli go s:"RN000006"
A 31 13 engine run.launch s:"RN000006"
A 32 14 engine run.ended s:"RN000006"
#
# AND THE FOOTER, which is the point of having one: the outer group's last
# member is done, so its release runs — and the group is NOT done until that
# release has finished. §3.6 makes the footer block for exactly this reason, so
# that a following scene reallocating the same channels waits for them to be
# given back rather than racing it.
A 33 15 engine run.spawn s:"RN000001" s:"FTR91000" s:"RN000007"
A 34 16 engine run.launch s:"RN000007"
A 35 17 engine run.ended s:"RN000007"
#
# Only now is the scene over.
A 36 18 engine run.ended s:"RN000001"
